What is an IGBT Module?
IGBT (Insulated Gate Bipolar Transistor) Modules represent a critical class of power semiconductor devices that combine the high-input impedance of MOSFETs with the low conduction loss of bipolar transistors. These advanced components function as electronic switches that can efficiently handle very high power levels, making them indispensable in applications where both high voltage and high current switching are required. The fundamental advantage of IGBT modules lies in their ability to significantly reduce power loss during operation, which directly translates to improved energy efficiency and operational cost savings for end-users.
IGBT modules are engineered to operate in medium- to high-power applications such as industrial motor drives, traction systems for electric vehicles and rail transport, uninterruptible power supplies, and induction heating equipment. Large IGBT modules typically package multiple semiconductor devices in parallel configurations, enabling them to achieve extraordinary current-handling capabilities—often reaching hundreds of amperes—while maintaining blocking voltages up to 6,500 volts. This technical capability allows them to control power flows in the megawatt range, making them essential for modern power conversion systems.
